The author, columnist, and ANSI X3J3 C Standards committee member, Rex
Jaeschke asked me about a problem he was having with \raggedbottom in
a new book he is finishing up.  He found footnotes floating up to the
bottom of the text, rather than staying down at the bottom of the page
where his publisher wants them.

I traced the problem to the definition of \skip\footins in lplain.tex,
which is set to \bigskipamount.  By inserting a redefinition of that
value in the definition of \raggedbottom, I was able to solve his
problem.  The new definition is

\def\raggedbottom{\def\@textbottom{\vskip \z@ plus.0001fil}\let\@texttop\relax
                  \skip\footins=12pt plus 1fil}

where the 2nd line contains the only change I made.

The questions for this group are
        (1) is this a design oversight in the current LaTeX that should be
        fixed, or did Leslie Lamport intend that footnotes in raggedbottom
        float upwards?
        (2) what is LaTeX 3 going to do with this?

Here is a test file that illustrates the effect of my changes; comment
out the \raggedbottom definition to see how it works in the current
LaTeX 2.09.

